import{_ as t,o as a,c as r,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.2cFhd8Ux.js";const g=JSON.parse('{"title":"Engineering Software with AI — Syllabus","description":"","frontmatter":{"source":"SYLLABUS.md"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"SYLLABUS.md","filePath":"SYLLABUS.md"}'),o={name:"SYLLABUS.md"};function n(d,e,i,l,c,h){return a(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="engineering-software-with-ai--syllabus" tabindex="-1">Engineering Software with AI — Syllabus <a class="header-anchor" href="#engineering-software-with-ai--syllabus" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Engineering Software with AI — Syllabus&quot;">​</a></h1><p><strong>Engineering practice for software built with AI coding agents, on a $0 budget.</strong> University of Idaho · CS [4xxx], joint-listed with CS [5xxx] · 3 credits · 16 weeks · a 75-minute Seminar and a 75-minute Lab each week · prerequisite: CS 3383 Software Engineering.</p><hr><h2 id="1-course-description" tabindex="-1">1. Course description <a class="header-anchor" href="#1-course-description" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;1. Course description&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Coding agents now write much of the code, so the hard problems lie elsewhere: specifying what software should do, verifying that it does it, securing agents and the code they write, and judging whether any of it helps. This course teaches those problems as engineering practice. Its core habit is <strong>Specify · Delegate · Verify</strong> (from UT Austin&#39;s CS 378): before every task, decide what you specify, what you delegate to an agent, and how you will verify the result. You spend the semester in a real open-source codebase, measuring test adequacy, reviewing and securing AI-written code, and building and evaluating an LLM feature, and you learn to read the evidence and tell a measured effect from hype.</p><h2 id="2-who-this-course-is-for" tabindex="-1">2. Who this course is for <a class="header-anchor" href="#2-who-this-course-is-for" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;2. Who this course is for&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The course is for CS seniors who have completed CS 3383 and for graduate students, who enroll in CS [5xxx] and add graduate work (<a href="#6-graduate-requirements-cs-5xxx">section 6</a>). It builds on CS 3383&#39;s requirements, design, testing and team practices instead of re-teaching them, and asks what changes when agents write much of the code. The lower-division sibling course, <a href="https://boyu-zhang-uoi.github.io/building-apps-with-ai/" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Building Apps with AI</a>, teaches students of any major to build web apps with AI using the Safe Loop; it is not a prerequisite, and this course goes much further, into how agents work, large codebases, test adequacy, security and evals. You need a laptop with a browser and free accounts (<a href="./setup/">setup</a>); the required cost is $0 (<a href="#10-tools-and-cost">Tools and cost</a>).</p><h2 id="3-learning-outcomes" tabindex="-1">3. Learning outcomes <a class="header-anchor" href="#3-learning-outcomes" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;3. Learning outcomes&quot;">​</a></h2><p>By the end of the course, CS [4xxx] and CS [5xxx] students will be able to:</p><ul><li><strong>Explain</strong> how LLM coding agents work (model, context, tools, the loop, permissions, sandboxes), and run, read and extend a minimal one.</li><li><strong>Engineer context</strong> for agents: specifications, <code>AGENTS.md</code>, and tools exposed over MCP (the Model Context Protocol), and measure whether an agent follows them.</li><li><strong>Verify</strong> AI-written code with tests whose adequacy is measured (property-based testing, mutation testing) and enforced in continuous integration.</li><li><strong>Change large existing codebases</strong> safely: comprehend, characterize, refactor, and contribute changes that reviewers accept.</li><li><strong>Review</strong> code at scale: review AI-authored pull requests, and evaluate AI reviewers and static analysis against known defects.</li><li><strong>Secure</strong> AI-assisted development: threat-model agents, defend against prompt injection, and manage supply-chain, secret and permission risks.</li><li><strong>Build and evaluate</strong> LLM-based features with structured outputs and evals, including judge calibration, cost and latency.</li><li><strong>Evaluate evidence</strong> about AI in software engineering (benchmarks, productivity and quality studies) and act responsibly toward users, maintainers and licenses.</li></ul><p>CS [5xxx] students also:</p><ul><li><strong>Analyze and present</strong> research in AI for software engineering.</li><li><strong>Design and run</strong> a small empirical study and report it in a short paper.</li></ul><p>Every outcome is assessed through graded work: for example, you show that you can verify AI-written code in the week 7 lab, where you raise one <code>roomly</code> module&#39;s mutation score with tests you can justify and explain each surviving mutant, then again for your team&#39;s contribution at M2 and in a live check at the final defense (<a href="./assessment/">assessment guide</a>).</p><h2 id="4-weekly-schedule" tabindex="-1">4. Weekly schedule <a class="header-anchor" href="#4-weekly-schedule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;4. Weekly schedule&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Title</th><th>Seminar</th><th>Lab and assignment</th><th>Team project / graduate</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0</td><td><a href="./setup/">Setup</a></td><td>—</td><td>Accounts, the Student Developer Pack, API keys, a sandbox, local models; read the agent safety contract</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>1</td><td><a href="./weeks/01-agentic-engineering/">From Vibe Coding to Agentic Engineering</a></td><td>Specify · Delegate · Verify; LLMs for engineers; maintainers vs graders</td><td>Run and read <code>mini-agent</code>, then extend it; trace dissection of a real CLI agent</td><td>Sign the safety contract; team survey; grad: rank three papers</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td><a href="./weeks/02-inside-agents/">Inside Real Agents</a></td><td>Harness architecture; reading a large codebase</td><td>Onboard to the class codebase; onboarding report</td><td>Teams of 3–4; <code>TEAM_CHARTER.md</code></td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td><a href="./weeks/03-context-engineering/">Context Engineering</a></td><td><code>AGENTS.md</code>, rules, skills, MCP; context rot</td><td>MCP server from scratch; <code>AGENTS.md</code> A/B test</td><td>Grad: paper presentations begin</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td><a href="./weeks/04-securing-agents/">Securing Agents</a></td><td>Prompt injection; the lethal trifecta; sandboxes and secrets</td><td>Injection lab: attack, then defend; extend a defense; <code>THREAT_MODEL.md</code></td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td><a href="./weeks/05-spec-driven-development/">Spec-Driven Development</a></td><td>Requirements → design → tasks; EARS; specs that change</td><td><code>roomly</code>: spec and build recurring bookings; beat 2 changes the spec</td><td><strong>M0</strong> proposal</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td><a href="./weeks/06-tests-as-oracles/">Tests as Oracles</a></td><td>Test oracles; TDD with agents; property-based testing</td><td>Property suite for <code>roomly</code>; bug reports; fixes, regression test first</td><td>Grad: study protocol</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td><a href="./weeks/07-test-adequacy/">Measuring Test Adequacy</a></td><td>Coverage vs mutation score; equivalent mutants</td><td>Mutation testing of <code>roomly</code>; raise a module&#39;s score and explain the survivors</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td><a href="./weeks/08-legacy-code/">Legacy Code</a></td><td>Characterization tests, seams, refactoring with agents</td><td>Characterize and refactor <code>roomly</code>&#39;s god module; <strong>oral exam 1</strong></td><td><strong>M1</strong> first PR; peer evaluation 1; PR-round preparation</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td><a href="./weeks/09-review-at-scale/">Review at Scale</a></td><td>Reviewing AI-authored PRs; AI reviewers; CI gates</td><td>Review lab vs an AI reviewer, scored against a defect key; CI on the team fork</td><td><strong>PR round</strong> opens: one PR on another team&#39;s fork</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td><a href="./weeks/10-supply-chain/">Supply Chain and Secure Code</a></td><td>Insecure-but-working code; slopsquatting; secrets; SBOMs</td><td>Scan and fix, with a regression test; security review of the team&#39;s work</td><td>PR round closes: decide on the PRs your team received</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td><a href="./weeks/11-llm-features/">Building LLM Features</a></td><td>Structured outputs, validation, cost and latency</td><td><code>eval-harness</code>: a validated extractor; error analysis and one measured improvement</td><td>Grad: interim results</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td><a href="./weeks/12-evals/">Evals</a></td><td>Eval design; LLM-as-judge calibration; benchmark limits</td><td>Eval set, calibrated judge, offline CI gate; eval report</td><td><strong>M2</strong> major contribution; peer evaluation 2; optional upstream PRs may start; grad: critique</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td><a href="./weeks/13-parallel-agents/">Parallel and Background Agents</a></td><td>Worktrees, cloud agents, orchestration, governance</td><td>Three agents on three issues; team process retrospective with data</td><td>—</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td><a href="./weeks/14-evidence-and-responsibility/">Evidence and Responsibility</a></td><td>Structured debate: productivity, quality and skills evidence; maintainers&#39; policies; licensing</td><td><strong>Oral exam 2</strong>; final-report draft</td><td>Grad: paper draft</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td><a href="./weeks/15-ship-and-reflect/">Ship and Reflect</a></td><td>Where the field is going; retrospective (no quiz)</td><td>Integration sprint; defense dry run (ungraded)</td><td>Grad: study talks</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td><a href="./weeks/16-final-defense/">Final Defense</a> (finals week)</td><td>—</td><td><strong>Final defense</strong> in the 2-hour final exam slot: team demo, a live check, individual 🔴 AI-off questions</td><td>Final report, peer evaluation 3 and grad final paper, due the last day of finals week</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Work listed in a week is due before the next week&#39;s Seminar (default 11:59 pm the night before) unless the row says otherwise; in weeks 2–14 that Seminar opens with a 7-minute 🔴 AI-off quiz on the assignment.</p><p>Oral exams use that week&#39;s Lab and office-hour slots posted in week 1, with Lab time cancelled to match; nothing exam-like happens in week 15 (Regulation H-1-a).</p><h2 id="5-the-team-project" tabindex="-1">5. The team project <a class="header-anchor" href="#5-the-team-project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;5. The team project&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The team project runs from week 2 to finals week, in <strong>the class codebase</strong>: a class fork of a real, active open-source project, chosen by the instructor before the term (<a href="./project/class-codebase.html">The Class Codebase</a>). The default candidates are open-source coding agents, so that the codebase also teaches the course&#39;s subject. Teams have 3–4 members; CS [5xxx] students may be on mixed teams.</p><p>It has three tiers. The first is graded; the second is graded individually; the third is optional. Following Michigan&#39;s EECS 481 experience, the course grades your process, never whether a maintainer merges your work (<a href="./project/">The Team Project</a>).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>What</th><th>Graded as</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>1. Core work in your team fork</strong></td><td>Each team works in its own fork of the class fork. PRs go to the team fork, reviewed by teammates; grading uses a snapshot of the fork (including open branches and PRs) at each deadline</td><td>Milestones M0, M1, M2 and Final</td></tr><tr><td><strong>2. The PR round</strong> (weeks 9–10)</td><td>Every student opens one PR on another team&#39;s fork; the receiving team reviews it and decides</td><td><a href="./assessment/rubrics.html#the-pr-round">The PR round rubric</a>, in Labs and assignments</td></tr><tr><td><strong>3. Optional upstream PRs</strong> (weeks 12–15)</td><td>A change already reviewed in your team fork, offered to the parent project or a short pre-vetted list, only where the project&#39;s written policy allows disclosed AI assistance. Staff screen your first one</td><td>A small bonus if merged; never a penalty (<a href="./project/contribution-rules.html">contribution rules</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Milestone</th><th>Due</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>M0 proposal</strong></td><td>End of week 5 (before the week 6 Seminar)</td><td>15</td></tr><tr><td><strong>M1 first PR</strong></td><td>End of week 8 (before the week 9 Seminar)</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td><strong>M2 major contribution</strong></td><td>End of week 12 (before the week 13 Seminar)</td><td>40</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Final</strong> (report, repository snapshot, final defense)</td><td>Finals week</td><td>65</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Deliverables are defined once, in <a href="./project/milestones.html">Team Project Milestones</a>. Each milestone has team rows and individual rows, and <strong>peer evaluations on Canvas</strong> at M1, M2 and Final, with the contribution evidence in the repository, can adjust an individual&#39;s share of the team rows (<a href="./assessment/#team-grades-and-peer-evaluation">how</a>).</p><p><strong>Rules for the team project:</strong> work only on public open-source code and the course kits; no real personal data or secrets anywhere; follow the agent safety contract; never send course work to the parent project except through Tier 3.</p><h2 id="6-graduate-requirements-cs-5xxx" tabindex="-1">6. Graduate requirements (CS [5xxx]) <a class="header-anchor" href="#6-graduate-requirements-cs-5xxx" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;6. Graduate requirements (CS [5xxx])&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The catalog says it in UIdaho&#39;s usual words: &quot;Additional projects/assignments required for graduate credit.&quot; Concretely, CS [5xxx] students do everything above plus about 1.5 hours a week of graduate work (<a href="./graduate/">Graduate Work (CS [5xxx])</a>):</p><ul><li><strong>A paper presentation and a discussant role</strong> (one of each): a 20-minute presentation of a paper from the reading list&#39;s graduate seminar set, in a Seminar in weeks 3–14, scored on CU Boulder&#39;s rubric (content 30, value added 30, materials 20, leading discussion 20); as discussant for a classmate&#39;s paper, you prepare three questions. Every other student posts one question before each presentation (<a href="./graduate/paper-presentations.html">paper presentations</a>).</li><li><strong>A critique</strong> of one paper (1,000 words), or a &quot;tool experience report&quot; in which you run a research tool on your own use case, on Canvas before the week 13 Seminar (<a href="./graduate/#the-critique">critique</a>).</li><li><strong>An empirical study</strong>, alone or in a pair: a small replication or extension, at $0, from the course&#39;s list of replicable studies or an approved proposal. Protocol in week 6, interim results in week 11, a draft in week 14, a 10-minute talk in week 15, and a 4–6 page paper in ACM format in finals week. <strong>No human-subjects work</strong> (no studying classmates or maintainers) without IRB approval (<a href="./graduate/empirical-study.html">empirical study</a>).</li></ul><h2 id="7-assessment" tabindex="-1">7. Assessment <a class="header-anchor" href="#7-assessment" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;7. Assessment&quot;">​</a></h2><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>CS [4xxx]</th><th>CS [5xxx]</th><th>How it is graded</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Labs and assignments</td><td>35%</td><td>30%</td><td>Weeks 1–13, each 20 points (checkoff 5, assignment 15), lowest week dropped; plus the PR round, 20 points (<a href="./assessment/rubrics.html#labs-and-assignments">rubric</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Due-date quizzes</td><td>10%</td><td>5%</td><td>7 minutes, AI-free, weeks 2–14; lowest two dropped (<a href="./assessment/quizzes.html">quizzes</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Team project</td><td>30%</td><td>25%</td><td>M0 15 · M1 30 · M2 40 · Final 65 points, the same for both levels; for CS [4xxx] that is M0 3% · M1 6% · M2 8% · Final 13% (<a href="./project/milestones.html">milestones</a>; <a href="./assessment/rubrics.html#team-project-milestones">rubrics</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Oral exams (weeks 8 and 14)</td><td>15%</td><td>15%</td><td>Two 15-minute exams, 🔴 AI off, 7.5% each (<a href="./assessment/oral-exams.html">oral exams</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Reading responses and Seminar participation</td><td>10%</td><td>5%</td><td>150–250-word responses on Canvas, plus taking part in the Seminar (<a href="./assessment/rubrics.html#reading-responses-and-seminar-participation">rubric</a>)</td></tr><tr><td>Graduate work</td><td>—</td><td>20%</td><td>Presentation and discussant 6 · critique 3 · empirical study 11 (<a href="./graduate/">graduate/</a>)</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>The <a href="./assessment/">assessment guide</a> is part of this syllabus.</strong> It explains how points become your grade, drops, grace tokens, late work, team grade adjustments and regrades, and the rules on <a href="./assessment/#absences-and-make-up-work">attendance, absences and make-up work</a>, pass/fail, incompletes, academic integrity and accommodations. I go through it in the first Seminar.</p><p><strong>Graded on evidence.</strong> Your grade rests on your commit history, your <code>AI_LOG.md</code>, your tests, checks anyone can reproduce, and an honest account of what doesn&#39;t work. UT Austin&#39;s CS 378 puts it this way, and so does this course: &quot;Feature count, repository size, token spend, prompt volume, and visual polish do not substitute for demonstrated behavior, reproducible checks, controlled engineering decisions, or honest limitations&quot; (<a href="https://www.cs.utexas.edu/~witchel/378AC/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CS 378</a>). Naming a real limitation never costs points.</p><h3 id="letter-grades" tabindex="-1">Letter grades <a class="header-anchor" href="#letter-grades" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Letter grades&quot;">​</a></h3><p>The University of Idaho uses letter grades without plus or minus (Regulation E).</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Grade</th><th>Course percentage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>A</strong></td><td>90–100</td></tr><tr><td><strong>B</strong></td><td>80–89</td></tr><tr><td><strong>C</strong></td><td>70–79</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D</strong></td><td>60–69</td></tr><tr><td><strong>F</strong></td><td>below 60</td></tr></tbody></table><p>To earn a C or higher you must also have <strong>taken both oral exams and the final defense</strong> (<a href="./assessment/#letter-grades">details</a>).</p><h3 id="late-work" tabindex="-1">Late work <a class="header-anchor" href="#late-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Late work&quot;">​</a></h3><p>You have <strong>two grace tokens</strong>, each extending one individual deadline by 48 hours with no reason needed; each team also has one team token for M0, M1 or M2. After that, late work loses 10% of its points per day for up to three days. Tokens can&#39;t be used for quizzes, oral exams, the PR round, the final defense or other live events, which have make-up rules instead (<a href="./assessment/#absences-and-make-up-work">Absences and make-up work</a>), and no token can move work past the University&#39;s grade deadline. <strong>Running out of free credits is not a reason to be late</strong>, as UT Austin&#39;s course also says: switching to a fallback is part of the course. Full rules: <a href="./assessment/#late-work-and-grace-tokens">Late work and grace tokens</a>.</p><h2 id="8-ai-use-policy" tabindex="-1">8. AI use policy <a class="header-anchor" href="#8-ai-use-policy" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;8. AI use policy&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This course <strong>requires</strong> AI for code. The question is never &quot;how much AI did you use&quot; but &quot;can you explain, verify and take responsibility for what you shipped&quot;. The rule is the one Michigan&#39;s EECS 498 uses: <strong>if you can&#39;t explain it, you didn&#39;t build it</strong> (<a href="https://eecs498-aase.github.io/syllabus.html" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">EECS 498</a>). Every task is labeled with one of three levels, always in words as well as color.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>When</th><th>The complete list</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>🟢 <strong>AI on</strong></td><td>Building, exploring, testing, reviewing your own work</td><td>Writing and refactoring code; writing tests; exploring a codebase; configuring CI; running AI reviewers and analyzers; preparing slides and figures for a graduate presentation (disclosed on the last slide); analysis code for the graduate study (disclosed). Everything not listed below is 🟢 AI on</td></tr><tr><td>🟡 <strong>AI limited</strong></td><td>Documents where the decisions are yours</td><td><strong><code>SPEC.md</code>:</strong> you write the problem, the criteria and the out-of-scope list; an AI may critique it. <strong><code>DESIGN.md</code>:</strong> an agent may explore the codebase and propose options; the decisions and their reasons are yours. <strong><code>TASKS.md</code>:</strong> you split the design into tasks; an AI may critique the split. <strong><code>AGENTS.md</code>:</strong> you write it; an AI may critique it. <strong><code>THREAT_MODEL.md</code>:</strong> an AI may help brainstorm attacks; the analysis and decisions are yours. <strong>The team&#39;s final report:</strong> the AI may help with tables, figures and checking claims against the repository, but the limitations, reflection and contribution sections are written by the team (🔴). <strong>The graduate study</strong> (protocol, interim report and paper): analysis code may be AI-assisted and is disclosed; the research question, the design and the prose are yours (🔴). <strong>Any other document a brief marks 🟡 AI limited</strong> follows that brief. Log the help you used in <code>AI_LOG.md</code></td></tr><tr><td>🔴 <strong>AI off</strong></td><td>Checks of your understanding, and writing you sign</td><td>Due-date quizzes · oral exams and their retake · the final defense&#39;s individual questions · reading responses · reflections, including the reflection parts of the final report · peer evaluations · PR descriptions and review comments you sign (in the team fork, the PR round and upstream; an AI reviewer may run alongside you, and you may compare after writing your own) · your Seminar question for a graduate presentation · graduate critiques and discussant questions · the graduate study&#39;s prose · any other task a brief marks 🔴 AI off</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>If two labels ever seem to disagree,</strong> follow the stricter one and ask. Using AI on 🔴 AI-off work, or beyond what a 🟡 AI-limited label allows, is academic dishonesty under the University&#39;s Student Code of Conduct (<a href="./assessment/#academic-integrity">academic integrity</a>).</p><ul><li><strong>Disclose.</strong> Every repository has an <code>AI_LOG.md</code> (<a href="./templates/AI_LOG.html">template</a>): the sessions, tools and models, what was delegated, and what was verified and how. Commits made with AI help end with <code>Assisted-by: [tool and model]</code>. Every PR&#39;s description has an AI disclosure section.</li><li><strong>Stricter policies win.</strong> When you contribute to someone else&#39;s project, including the parent of the class codebase, its AI policy overrides the course&#39;s whenever it is stricter (<a href="./project/contribution-rules.html">contribution rules</a>).</li><li><strong>Credit others.</strong> Code, text or data from a person, a paper or a website is credited where you use it, with its license.</li></ul><h2 id="9-agent-safety-and-data" tabindex="-1">9. Agent safety and data <a class="header-anchor" href="#9-agent-safety-and-data" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;9. Agent safety and data&quot;">​</a></h2><h3 id="the-agent-safety-contract" tabindex="-1">The agent safety contract <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-agent-safety-contract" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The agent safety contract&quot;">​</a></h3><p>You read the <a href="./setup/safety-contract.html">agent safety contract</a> in week 0 and sign it on Canvas in week 1. Each rule starts the week it is taught, and the course builds protections around you: Codespaces, kits with an approval gate and a workspace guard, a worthless canary instead of a real key in the injection lab, and graded work in a class fork rather than maintainers&#39; repositories. In short:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>In practice</th><th>Taught in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. <strong>The data rule</strong></td><td>Free tiers see only public open-source code and the course kits (<a href="#the-data-rule">below</a>)</td><td>Weeks 0–1</td></tr><tr><td>2. <strong>Approvals stay on</strong></td><td>Every command and every write needs your approval unless you allowlisted that exact, read-only command. No auto-approve, &quot;yolo&quot; or skip-permissions modes; an agent never edits its own permissions</td><td>Week 1</td></tr><tr><td>3. <strong>Untrusted code runs in a sandbox</strong></td><td>Repositories you didn&#39;t write are opened only in a Codespace or a dev container, cloned from their URL</td><td>Weeks 2 and 4</td></tr><tr><td>4. <strong>No real secrets near an agent</strong></td><td>An agent session that reads untrusted text has nothing worth stealing in reach</td><td>Weeks 1 and 4</td></tr><tr><td>5. <strong>Scoped tokens only</strong></td><td>Fine-grained, one repository, fewest permissions, an expiry date</td><td>Weeks 4 and 13</td></tr><tr><td>6. <strong>Spending caps at zero</strong></td><td>No payment method on course accounts; nothing metered with a cap above zero or the lowest value allowed; never an agentic workflow whose per-run cap could exceed a free allowance</td><td>Weeks 0, 9 and 13</td></tr><tr><td>7. <strong>Save points before and after</strong></td><td>Commit before and after every agent task, on a branch or worktree; <code>main</code> protected</td><td>Weeks 1 and 13</td></tr><tr><td>8. <strong>Read before you adopt</strong></td><td>An MCP server, skill, rules file, Action or package is someone else&#39;s code until you&#39;ve read it; check and pin packages</td><td>Weeks 3 and 10</td></tr><tr><td>9. <strong>Verify, and say how</strong></td><td>Claims tagged observed or inferred; only observed ones count as verified</td><td>Weeks 1 and 6–7</td></tr><tr><td>10. <strong>No unattended agents in other people&#39;s spaces</strong></td><td>No agent posts, comments or opens anything in a repository you don&#39;t own without you reading it first</td><td>Weeks 9 and 12–15</td></tr><tr><td>11. <strong>Disclose AI help</strong></td><td><code>AI_LOG.md</code>, <code>Assisted-by:</code> trailers and the PR template&#39;s AI section</td><td>Week 1</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The contract adds three project rules: no real personal data, no human-subjects research without IRB approval, and nothing you&#39;d miss if an agent deleted it.</p><h3 id="the-data-rule" tabindex="-1">The data rule <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-data-rule" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The data rule&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Free tiers may use your prompts to train their models. <strong>Use them only on public open-source code and the course kits; never on personal data, secrets, or code you may not share.</strong> That includes your own grades and feedback, classmates&#39; writing, unpublished research code, and anything an employer owns.</p><h3 id="privacy-of-your-work" tabindex="-1">Privacy of your work <a class="header-anchor" href="#privacy-of-your-work" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Privacy of your work&quot;">​</a></h3><p>Your code is public on purpose: the team forks and the PR round only work in the open. Your grades and personal writing are not. University policy treats grades and graded work as private (FSH 4700 F-6, and FSH 2600, which applies FERPA, the US federal student-records law), so:</p><ul><li><strong>In public repositories,</strong> under your GitHub username: code, tests, CI, <code>SPEC.md</code>, <code>DESIGN.md</code>, <code>TASKS.md</code>, <code>AGENTS.md</code>, <code>THREAT_MODEL.md</code>, <code>EVAL_PLAN.md</code>, <code>AI_LOG.md</code>, PR descriptions and code review comments.</li><li><strong>On Canvas only:</strong> reading responses, reflections, peer evaluations, the final report, graduate critiques, discussant notes and study papers, quiz and oral-exam results, the signed safety contract, and all grades and feedback.</li><li><strong>Never commit</strong> grades, feedback, reflections, peer evaluations, classmates&#39; real names (use GitHub usernames), your student ID, or real personal data about anyone.</li></ul><p>Details: <a href="./assessment/#privacy-of-your-work">Privacy of your work</a>.</p><h2 id="10-tools-and-cost" tabindex="-1">10. Tools and cost <a class="header-anchor" href="#10-tools-and-cost" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;10. Tools and cost&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Required cost: <strong>$0</strong>, from free tiers, the GitHub Student Developer Pack, open-source tools and a department lab machine that serves open-weight models through Ollama to the class (one shared queue). Every layer (chat, IDE agent, CLI agent, background agents, AI review, an LLM API for the kits, local models, CI, static analysis, secret scanning, testing, evals, sandboxes) has a primary free tool and two fallbacks, listed with their current limits in <a href="./TOOLS.html">Tool Handout</a>. Department-funded API credits are an optional upgrade, never assumed. Paying for a tool is never needed for full marks.</p><p>Two facts shape the design. Copilot&#39;s student offer has a small model allowance, so use it lightly (completions, light chat), never as your main agent. Heavy agent work is spread across several separate free allowances and the lab machine. The kits read three environment variables, <code>LLM_BASE_URL</code>, <code>LLM_API_KEY</code> and <code>LLM_MODEL</code>, for any OpenAI-compatible API, so switching provider (or moving to the lab machine) is a configuration change, not a code change.</p><p>Free tiers change without notice. When a tool stops working for you, switching to the fallback is part of the course: see <a href="./resources/troubleshooting.html">Troubleshooting</a>.</p><h2 id="11-if-you-fall-behind" tabindex="-1">11. If you fall behind <a class="header-anchor" href="#11-if-you-fall-behind" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;11. If you fall behind&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Each week builds on the last, so act early. Do each lab&#39;s <strong>Minimum to keep up</strong> box first. Tell your team early if you&#39;re behind on team work: a short message now beats a bad peer evaluation later. Office hours exist for &quot;I don&#39;t understand what the agent did&quot;.</p>',59)])])}const p=t(o,[["render",n]]);export{g as __pageData,p as default};
